    "prompt_templates": {
        "phenotype_synonyms_prompt": "You are a medical expert helping to identify alternative terms and synonyms for phenotypes/diseases.\n\nGiven the following phenotype: {phenotype}\n\nGenerate between 0 and 10 relevant synonyms or alternative terms that could be used to search for scientific literature about this phenotype. Include:\n- Alternative medical terminology\n- Common abbreviations\n- Related clinical terms\n- Different ways the condition might be described in literature\n\nIMPORTANT: \n- If the phenotype is already very specific and well-defined, you may return fewer synonyms (even 0).\n- Only include terms that are semantically equivalent or very closely related.\n- Do NOT include broader categories, subtypes, or related but distinct conditions.\n\nReturn your answer in this exact XML format:\n<response>\n<synonyms>\n<synonym>term 1</synonym>\n<synonym>term 2</synonym>\n...\n</synonyms>\n</response>\n\nIf you think no synonyms are needed, return:\n<response>\n<synonyms>\n</synonyms>\n</response>",
        "novelty_assessment_prompt": "You are a scientific literature expert evaluating the genetic association between {gene_name} and {phenotype}.\n\nBelow is a list of scientific articles about {gene_name} and/or {phenotype}:\n\n{alias_note}{dismiss_note}\n\n{articles_list}\n\nClassify the gene\u2013phenotype relationship into ONE of four categories:\n\n1. **Established**  \n   Strong, replicated evidence that variants in {gene_name} CAUSE {phenotype}. Multiple clinical or mechanistic studies support a clear causal role.\n   Research used to prove this category has to be in Human.\n\n2. **Existing**\n   At least one credible study reports that variation in {gene_name} contributes to or is associated with {phenotype}, but evidence is limited or not definitive.  \n   Also choose **Existing** when variants in {gene_name} are associated with a phenotype that is a well-recognized precursor, subtype, mechanism, or risk factor strongly linked to {phenotype}.\n   Research used to prove this category has to be in Human.\n\n3. **Hypothesized**  \n   Literature suggests a possible relationship based on biology, even without direct evidence from genetic variants.  \n   Includes expression, functional data, pathway links, or relevant animal models.  \n   Excludes cases where gene and phenotype are merely co-mentioned without relevance.\n\n4. **Novel**  \n   No evidence that variants or biological activity of {gene_name} influence {phenotype}.  \n   Co-mentions are incidental and provide no logical basis.\n\nYour task:\n1. Read the article titles and abstracts.  \n2. Choose the best category.  \n3. Provide a brief justification (3\u20135 sentences).  \n4. Cite up to 10 relevant PMIDs from the provided list only.\n\nReturn your answer in **this exact XML format**:\n\n<response>\n  <verdict>Established|Existing|Hypothesized|Novel</verdict>\n  <justification>Your brief explanation (3\u20135 sentences)</justification>\n  <pmids>\n    <pmid>12345678</pmid>\n    ...\n  </pmids>\n</response>\n\nAdditional rules:\n- If uncertain between two categories, choose the *less novel* one.  \n- Hypothesized can apply without phenotype measurements if the gene\u2019s biology is directly relevant.  \n- Think step by step internally: if the studies used to prove \"Existing\" or \"Established\" are not in human but in an animal or in vitro experiments, downgrade to \"Hypothesized\".\n- Maximum 10 PMIDs."
